    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d923-a1">

                <pb facs="0946" n="858" sameAs="frus:frus1904/pg858"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 1.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Finch to Señor
                            Romeu.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Montevideo,
                                Uruguay</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1904-05-17"><hi rendition="#italic">May 17,
                        1904</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: In reply to dispatches from me in relation
                    to the arrest and subsequent release of Louis E. Hufnagel at Paysandu, Uruguay,
                    for failure to report for military duty in the national guard of this Republic,
                    the honorable Secretary of State at Washington, under date of April 8, 1904,
                    says:</p>
                <p>“I have to say that, although by section 1993 of the Revised Statutes of the
                    United States children born abroad of American fathers are citizens of the
                    United States, the law can not be so construed as to exempt them from the
                    allegiance due to the country of their birth so long as they remain within its
                    territory, provided that by the law of the country where they are born and
                    reside such children are citizens of that country.”</p>
                <p>I am, etc,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">William R. Finch</hi>,</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
